{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbnZXoYEZudfzL994J3k", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Lists** are mutable and **tuples** are immutable.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutability | Can add, remove, or change items | Fixed once created |\n| Methods | `append`, `remove`, `sort`, and so on | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster to create |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** a collection that grows, shrinks, or is reordered, usually of items of the same kind, such as a list of users.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record whose positions have meaning, such as `(x, y)`, `(name, age)`, or multiple return values. Tuples can also be dict keys, such as `{(0, 0): \"origin\"}`.\n\n**Caveats**\n- A one-item tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`. Without it, `(1)` is just the integer 1.\n- Tuples are only shallowly immutable. If a tuple holds a list, you can still mutate that list: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(3)` works."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7215, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7215}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ff19ca3b-cb13-4ec7-89d5-0c002e9a2685", "uuid": "b6419b5d-7aa4-4693-aedd-5f78f6e50dd1", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:53:09.247Z", "requ